WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
It is time for Drew's News.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.390
Bringing you that good news pills

3
00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:05.824
straight from Capitol Hill.

4
00:00:05.824 --> 00:00:07.750
And with me at the desk,

5
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.400
co-president of this tiny news nation,

6
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.909
it's Rossy-Ross Mathews!

7
00:00:11.909 --> 00:00:12.773
Hello!

8
00:00:12.773 --> 00:00:14.150
(crowd cheering)

9
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.225
Hello, everybody! (giggles)

10
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:21.090
Okay, this season, we're taking our new show on the road.

11
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.290 line:15%
And today, it is beyond an honor

12
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.240 line:15%
to be in our nation's capital, Washington, DC.

13
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.860 line:15%
Ross, did you ever think

14
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.490
that we would be reporting from the mall?

15
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:34.740
The Lincoln Memorial?

16
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.520
Never in a million years.

17
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.429
This is crazy, but hello DC!

18
00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:42.590
Hello, DC!
(crowd cheering)

19
00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:43.850
Amazing.

20
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.590
All right, let's get to our first news story.

21
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:48.570
First up, the White House is just down the road

22
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.370
and while so many presidents have come and gone,

23
00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.210
decorated and redecorated,

24
00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.050
they all did agree on one thing.

25
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.450
A home is not a home without a fluffy thing in it.

26
00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.920
Thank you!
And Presidents

27
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.100
are just like us.

28
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.280
They love their pets.

29
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.560
Ross, you love your doggies.

30
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.393
Hello?

31
00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:04.226
I have three of them.

32
00:01:04.226 --> 00:01:05.110
That's right, and I have two.

33
00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.410
And The New York Times did a deep dive

34
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.962
on first pets throughout White House history.

35
00:01:09.962 --> 00:01:14.030 line:15%
Lincoln himself had dogs, cats, and a pair of goat brothers.

36
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:14.863 line:15%
What?

37
00:01:14.863 --> 00:01:15.696 line:15%
And a turkey.

38
00:01:15.696 --> 00:01:17.204 line:15%
Oh, cause you just throw the turkey in.

39
00:01:17.204 --> 00:01:18.185 line:15%
( Drew imitates turkey gobbling)

40
00:01:18.185 --> 00:01:19.018 line:15%
(Ross laughing)

41
00:01:19.018 --> 00:01:22.160 line:15%
LBJ was smitten with his little mutt, Yuki,

42
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.700 line:15%
who sat on his lap when he signed bills.

43
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.533
I love that.

44
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:26.630
I know. Me too.

45
00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.750
Good luck. Good omen.

46
00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.690
And in fact, 31 out of 46 presidents had dogs.

47
00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:35.880
But, that's just the tip of the pet iceberg.

48
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.290
Taft had a cow named Pauline,

49
00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.090
and she grazed the south lawn

50
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.590
and provided fresh milk and butter.

51
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.563
This is utterly fascinating. (laughing)

52
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.079
No, not with the udders.

53
00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:51.070
I can't help it.

54
00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.240
It's where my mind goes.

55
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.480
You're milking it for all for all it's worth.

56
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.420
I always have.

57
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.580
This one I really like.

58
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.630
Fun fact, Coolidge had a raccoon named Rebecca.

59
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:01.512
What?

60
00:02:01.512 --> 00:02:03.390
Who was meant to be Thanksgiving dinner,

61
00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.870
but became BFFs with the first lady just in time.

62
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.940
By the way, raccoon?

63
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.490
For dinner?

64
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:11.590
Thanksgiving?

65
00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.830
JFK had a pony named Macaroni,

66
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:17.280
and Woodrow Wilson kept the lawn trim and tidy

67
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.960
with a soft and fluffy flock of sheep.

68
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.860
In a Ross Matthews White House,

69
00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.850
who would be your menagerie?

70
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:24.683
Hello?

71
00:02:24.683 --> 00:02:25.977
The first chihuahua, the second chihuahua,

72
00:02:25.977 --> 00:02:27.870
and the third chihuahua.

73
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.090
I feel like they kind of already are that.

74
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:30.923
You know what?

75
00:02:30.923 --> 00:02:32.030
They're treated like that.

76
00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.070
I bet. Yeah, that's true.

77
00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:37.328
Well, next we've got a little taste of DC in Food News.

78
00:02:37.328 --> 00:02:40.023
(crowd cheering)

79
00:02:40.023 --> 00:02:41.540
Nom, nom, nom, nom, nom, nom, nom, nom.

80
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.710
We've got a legendary DC dish

81
00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.060
that's been prepared the same way for 63 years.

82
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.395
So you know they are doing it right.

83
00:02:48.395 --> 00:02:51.260
It's the delicious chili from Ben's Chili Bowl.

84
00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.700
And this family business is run by the Ali family,

85
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.080 line:15%
and they've kept their chili recipe secret since 1958.

86
00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.250 line:15%
That's what I call secret service.

87
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.083 line:15%
Yeah.

88
00:03:00.083 --> 00:03:02.100 line:15%
They've served it to everyone from Duke Ellington,

89
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.077 line:15%
Barack Obama, even Martin Luther king Jr.

90
00:03:05.077 --> 00:03:06.850 line:15%
And we have some right here!

91
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:08.270 line:15%
Now they're serving us.

92
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:09.310
Look, Drew.

93
00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:10.643
Chili!
I can't believe it.

94
00:03:10.643 --> 00:03:12.020
I'm so excited.

95
00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.910
And we love chili.

96
00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:14.743
Ready?

97
00:03:17.514 --> 00:03:19.660
Okay. Mm-hm.
Oh my god.

98
00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.550
All right. I see you, Ben.

99
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:22.843
This is good.

100
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.340
Yeah.

101
00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.173
OMG.

102
00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.590
Oh my gosh. I wish I could share with you,

103
00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:31.900
but that's like not allowed.

104
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.810
Sorry.

105
00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.330
Oh my gosh, this is the best chili I've ever had.

106
00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:36.310
Yum!

107
00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.650
Okay, next.

108
00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.040
Since we're in DC, Rossy,

109
00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.210
we must discuss some of the biggest issues

110
00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:42.610
facing our nation.

111
00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:47.140
It's time to tackle some topics, just a notch down from hot,

112
00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.926
in a segment we like to call Warm Topics.

113
00:03:49.926 --> 00:03:51.950
(crowd cheering)
(upbeat music)

114
00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:52.830
We're not controversial around here.

115
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.750
No, this is just nice and warm.

116
00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.760
All right, let's kick it off with warm topic number one.

117
00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.240
The Wall Street journal reports.

118
00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:00.911
You know the smiley face emoji?

119
00:04:00.911 --> 00:04:01.744
Yes.

120
00:04:01.744 --> 00:04:03.797
Okay, well it's causing some generational debate

121
00:04:03.797 --> 00:04:06.270 line:15%
because older people think the smiley emoji

122
00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:07.830 line:15%
is just a smiley emoji.

123
00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.350 line:15%
But gen Zers, some are saying they see it as sarcastic,

124
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.020 line:15%
even passive aggressive.

125
00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.240 line:15%
Like when they send it to you,

126
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.820 line:15%
they're not really sending you a smiley face.

127
00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.550
They're sending you this, ugh.

128
00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.060
And it's really sent me into a spiral

129
00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:20.400
because all these times I'm thinking

130
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.640 line:15%
people are sending me a smiley face,

131
00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.240 line:15%
they're really rolling their eyes at me.

132
00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.600 line:15%
Gosh, this is what the young people call cheugy.

133
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.990 line:15%
Like, it's just so over already, you know?

134
00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.070 line:15%
That's right!

135
00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.310 line:15%
The smiley face is apparently cheugy.

136
00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.820 line:15%
I don't think there's anything to misinterpret here.

137
00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:35.280
It's a smiley face, you know?

138
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.060
Everything in life is getting so sensitive.

139
00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.240
It's like, we're just, you know,

140
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.120
now it's the smiley face emoji we have to worry about?

141
00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:44.380
Like, what next?

142
00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:45.820
Just keep it simple.

143
00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:46.980
Even if you're rolling your eyes at me,

144
00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.260
I'm gonna think it's a smiley face.

145
00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.030
Don't send me a smiley face and be passive aggressive.

146
00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.503
You just give it to me good.

147
00:04:52.503 --> 00:04:54.050
Okay, that's a promise.

148
00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.010
Okay, good.
I will.

149
00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:56.390
(Drew laughing)

150
00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:57.820
All right, warm topic number two.

151
00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:58.930
The Washington Post reports

152
00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:01.840
that despite the workplace moving to Zoom,

153
00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.412
coworkers are still hooking up like crazy.

154
00:05:05.412 --> 00:05:06.245
(crowd oohing)

155
00:05:06.245 --> 00:05:07.110
Yes!

156
00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:10.660
50% of workers say they currently have an office crush,

157
00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:15.660 line:15%
and 25% started or continued in office romance this year.

158
00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.250 line:15%
Even despite Zoom.

159
00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:19.210 line:15%
So Drew, I gotta know.

160
00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.740 line:15%
When it comes to workplace hoochie coochie,

161
00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.130 line:15%
are you for it or against it?

162
00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:24.660
I am for it.

163
00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.220 line:15%
You know, I've dipped my pen in the company ink

164
00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.129 line:15%
a time or two.

165
00:05:28.129 --> 00:05:28.962 line:15%
What?

166
00:05:28.962 --> 00:05:29.795 line:15%
Well, you know what?

167
00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:30.650 line:15%
Here's the thing.

168
00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.990 line:15%
How many of you spend almost as much time at work

169
00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.590 line:15%
as you do with your friends and family?

170
00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.380 line:15%
Or home life?
Yes. Yes.

171
00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.560
You know, I have fallen in love at work.

172
00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:42.500
I'm not alone.

173
00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:45.200
Look at how many actors have met on a film

174
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.440
and fallen in love.

175
00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.390
I mean-
Yes.

176
00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.980
I think a lot of coworkers in an office,

177
00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:53.730
kids at high school, when you are confined together,

178
00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:54.673
this is what happens.

179
00:05:54.673 --> 00:05:56.680
You get to know each other.

180
00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.660
And I always hope that it's through getting to know someone

181
00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.249
that you really fall in love with them,

182
00:06:01.249 --> 00:06:04.700
rather than just looks because you should fall in love

183
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:05.720
from the inside out.

184
00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:07.291
I think it's more everlasting.

185
00:06:07.291 --> 00:06:09.710
So, you know, maybe those hot hookups

186
00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:11.230
that are all about physical attraction,

187
00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.953
those may be a little worrisome in the workplace.

188
00:06:13.953 --> 00:06:16.520
But then I also think it's kind of like,

189
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.620
it was fun, it was surface, move on, you know?

190
00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.960
I hear you, and I think if you find the one

191
00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.230
and there's that thing going on,

192
00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.590
you should do everything in your power to make it happen.

193
00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.718
Including finding another job.

194
00:06:27.718 --> 00:06:29.120
(Drew laughing)

195
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.060
Because I think if that's all happening in the workplace,

196
00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.830
it's super uncomfy for everybody else you work with.

197
00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.680
So if it's just a fling, fine, but if it's something more,

198
00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.590
you just have to make it work in real life,

199
00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.240
and I don't think that's a long-term solution.

200
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.430 line:15%
I also think if you do take the risk of a work romance,

201
00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.300 line:15%
you have got to be an adult about what happens

202
00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.640 line:15%
if it doesn't work out.

203
00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.060
Yes!
And that's kind of on you.

204
00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.350
Yeah, well actually it's on you

205
00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:54.455
'cause you're the one that would do this.

206
00:06:54.455 --> 00:06:55.730
(Drew and Ross laugh)

207
00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:56.563
And I have.

208
00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.890
And who knows, it could happen again.

209
00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.759
Ross, you coming over to my side?

210
00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:03.837
(crowd whooping)

211
00:07:03.837 --> 00:07:05.340
I'm gonna have more of this chili and think about it.

212
00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.843
Ross, I would say this.

213
00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:11.350
Any girl, man, anyone on the planet

214
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.530
would be lucky to be with you.

215
00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:14.363
Drew!

216
00:07:14.363 --> 00:07:15.196
It's true.

217
00:07:15.196 --> 00:07:16.029
Samesies.

218
00:07:16.029 --> 00:07:16.862
I love you.

219
00:07:16.862 --> 00:07:17.695
I love you more.

220
00:07:17.695 --> 00:07:20.686 line:15%
(upbeat fun music)

